  Proof:

  Lemma 1 (prefix invariant): after the loops, for all 0≤i≤n, 0≤j≤m, pre[i][j] = Σ_{x=1..i} Σ_{y=1..j} a[x][y], with pre[i][0]=pre[0][j]=0.

  Induction on i then j: base i=0 or j=0 zero. Step: the set of cells in rectangle [1..i]×[1..j] = [1..i-1]×[1..j] ∪ [1..i]×[1..j-1] ∪ {(i,j)} minus intersection [1..i-1]×[1..j-1]; by IE the counts are correct, giving the recurrence. So the computed value equals the true sum. Standard.

  Lemma 2 (query formula): the answer A = Σ_{i=r1..r2} Σ_{j=c1..c2} a[i][j] = pre[r2][c2] - pre[r1-1][c2] - pre[r2][c1-1] + pre[r1-1][c1-1].

  Proof: each cell (i,j) with 1≤i≤r2, 1≤j≤c2 contributes to pre[r2][c2] with +1. Indicator: [i≤r2][j≤c2] - [i≤r1-1][j≤c2] - [i≤r2][j≤c1-1] + [i≤r1-1][j≤c1-1] = ([i≤r2]-[i≤r1-1])([j≤c2]-[j≤c1-1]) = [r1≤i≤r2][c1≤j≤c2]. So coefficient is 1 exactly for cells in the query rectangle and 0 otherwise. Hence sum equals A.

  Then theorem: output line k equals A_k for each query. Since the problem guarantees r1≤r2, c1≤c2, ranges valid, formula applies. Also note the sum is uniquely determined; the program prints exactly one integer per query line with no extra output → matches judge's token comparison.

